  "result": "All three tests pass with `python -m pytest -q` (Python 3.12 venv). The implementation is in `netgraph/graph.py`.\n\n**Approach chosen:** Approach A, adjacency sets, one `set[int]` per node plus a running edge counter. The spec's interface is dominated by sparse-graph operations (sorted `neighbors`, BFS over neighbours, an edge count), and sets give O(1) edge lookup and duplicate detection anyway, so the matrix's only advantage (compactness on dense graphs) didn't buy anything here while costing O(n\u00b2) memory.\n\nNotes on the implementation:\n- Self-loops are handled by adding `v` to `u`'s set; the symmetric add is a no-op, so the loop counts once in `edge_count` and shows up once in `neighbors`.\n- BFS visits neighbours in sorted order and reconstructs the path via a parent map, so results are deterministic.\n- Out-of-range ids (including negatives) raise `IndexError` on every node-taking method.",
